688 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 497 0 R
/E (Monthly maintenance costs fluctuate unpredictably between $0.05M and $0.15M. While average load weight occasionally correlates with cost spikes, a distinct lag effect disrupts a perfect correlation. This lag represents a temporary "safety window" where newly repaired trucks briefly resist heavy payload stress before cumulative wear triggers the next breakdown.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
689 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 497 0 R
/E (Crucially, this safety window is alarmingly short: 74% of maintenance expenses go to trucks re-entering the shop within 30 to 60 days. Notably, the aging 2013 models alone consume 33% of the fleet's total maintenance budget. This confirms that immediate failures stem from poor initial repair quality, heavily compounded by critical fleet depreciation rather than normal operational wear.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
690 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 497 0 R
/E (Recommendations: To eliminate rapid re-entry costs, implement strict workshop warranties and dynamically schedule preventive maintenance based on cumulative payload weight hauled. Additionally, collaborate with the Finance department to structure a phased installment plan for acquiring new trucks)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
